## Git & PR Workflow

### Base branch

This worktree was forked from `{{.Base}}`. Use `{{.Base}}` as the baseline for all diffs and "what changed?" lookups:

- `git diff {{.Base}}...HEAD`
- `git log {{.Base}}..HEAD`
- `git diff origin/{{.Base}}...HEAD` (against the remote)

Don't default to `master`/`main` if `{{.Base}}` is something else.
{{if and .PRBase (ne .PRBase .Base)}}
**Dual-base flow:** this repo branches from `{{.Base}}` but PRs target `{{.PRBase}}`. The PR target is separate from the diff baseline; for local comparison stick with `{{.Base}}`.{{end}}

### Commits

Use Conventional Commits: `type(scope): description`. Types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`, `revert`. Scope is the package/service name, optional but preferred. Explain *why* in the body only when it isn't obvious; the diff shows *what*.

### Branch naming

After confirming the task, rename the branch to a Conventional Branch name. The directory path doesn't matter; only the branch name does. `git branch -m <new-name>`.

Format: `<prefix>/<task-title>`{{if .ClickUp}}, or `<prefix>/#<task_id>/<task-title>` when there's a tracker task{{end}}.

Prefixes describe the **task**: `feature` (new feature), `fix` (production bug fix), `hotfix` (urgent prod fix), `epic` (umbrella task), `chore` (refactor, docs, deps, tooling, cleanup). Title is short lowercase kebab-case.

### Pull Requests

When the task is complete:
1. Push the branch to origin.
2. Open the PR: `gh pr create --assignee {{.User.Name | printf "%s"}} --base {{if .PRBase}}{{.PRBase}}{{else}}{{.Base}}{{end}}`. Default to ready-for-review; use `--draft` only when unsure it's ready (say why).
{{- if .ClickUp}}
3. Reference the task ID in the PR title or body (e.g. `#<task_id>`).
{{- end}}
4. Open it in the browser: `gh pr view --web`.
5. Keep PR descriptions concise. No test-plan section unless asked.
